\details{
The data cover the 32 Mexican states. The observation year is 2024
and is recorded here rather than in a separate dataset column.

The program aims to improve the monetary income of households of
Mexican people with permanent disabilities. Its target population
comprises Mexican citizens by birth or naturalization with permanent
disabilities who currently reside in Mexico and meet one of the
following conditions:
\itemize{
\item Are aged 0–29 years.
\item Are aged 30–64 years and live in Indigenous or Afro-Mexican
municipalities or localities, or in municipalities or localities
with high or very high marginalization.
\item Are aged 30–64 years and reside in a state whose government has
signed an agreement to universalize the pension for people with
permanent disabilities.
}

Monetary support consists of pension payments. In-kind support
consists of vouchers for rehabilitation services for beneficiaries
aged 0–17 years.

All four numeric variables count people. The support variables do
not measure monetary amounts, payments, vouchers, or rehabilitation
sessions. Monetary-support and in-kind-support counts are not added,
to avoid double-counting the served population.

Preparation preserved state names, row order, and numeric values,
including zero values for in-kind support. Geographic
identifiers cve_ent and iso_3166_2 were added during preparation;
they are not columns in the source report table.

INEGI codes follow \emph{Clasificaciones del Censo de Población y
Vivienda 2020}, printed pp. 33–34:
\url{https://www.inegi.org.mx/contenidos/productos/prod_serv/contenidos/espanol/bvinegi/productos/nueva_estruc/702825198701.pdf}.

ISO 3166-2 codes were checked against the UK Permanent Committee
on Geographical Names' \emph{Mexico: Toponymic Factfile}, March 2025,
pp. 4–6, which cites ISO's Mexico entry:
\url{https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/67eaae480678ace40a7f27a4/Mexico_Toponymic_Factfile.pdf}.

Source names Coahuila, Michoacán, and Veracruz were matched to
Coahuila de Zaragoza, Michoacán de Ocampo, and Veracruz de
Ignacio de la Llave, respectively.

